 % Source: Reviewer A, Detailed 1.
-\reviewcomment{1.1} \textbf{1. Novelty} SEDAR is the closest predecessor because it addresses the same high-level problem of transferring existing DBMS test cases into fuzzing seeds. SEDAR already processes complete multi-statement test cases. It sequentially executes the source test, captures a referenced source-side sub-schema for each statement, translates individual statements, and restores their original order. Its mutability refinement also temporarily hides regions unsupported by the fuzzer parser during mutation and restores them before sending the resulting SQL to the target DBMS.
+\reviewcomment{1.4} \textbf{1. Novelty} SEDAR is the closest predecessor because it addresses the same high-level problem of transferring existing DBMS test cases into fuzzing seeds. SEDAR already processes complete multi-statement test cases. It sequentially executes the source test, captures a referenced source-side sub-schema for each statement, translates individual statements, and restores their original order. Its mutability refinement also temporarily hides regions unsupported by the fuzzer parser during mutation and restores them before sending the resulting SQL to the target DBMS.

 MORPH should therefore not be positioned as the first whole-test-case transfer framework or the first system to adapt transferred seeds to downstream fuzzers. Nevertheless, MORPH provides clear technical advances over SEDAR. In particular, it replaces independent statement-level translation with an explicit AQT representation, forward read-after-write dependencies, evolving target-side context, dialect-evidence-guided instantiation, and parser/execution-feedback repair. SEDAR does not maintain translated target context across statements and explicitly leaves target-side execution-feedback regeneration as future work. I consider these differences meaningful.

