-\reviewcomment{1.3} \textbf{3. Semantic Correctness} The paper explicitly states that its evaluation targets fuzzing-seed quality rather than source-to-target result equivalence. This is a reasonable design choice. For fuzzing, a transferred seed primarily needs to be executable on the target DBMS, retain useful SQL structures, and be compatible with downstream mutation. Full semantic equivalence is not necessarily required for this purpose.
+\reviewcomment{1.6} \textbf{3. Semantic Correctness} The paper explicitly states that its evaluation targets fuzzing-seed quality rather than source-to-target result equivalence. This is a reasonable design choice. For fuzzing, a transferred seed primarily needs to be executable on the target DBMS, retain useful SQL structures, and be compatible with downstream mutation. Full semantic equivalence is not necessarily required for this purpose.

 However, the metric named ``Semantic Correctness'' only checks whether a seed executes on the target DBMS without a runtime error. This establishes execution validity rather than semantic equivalence. The distinction is important because repair operations such as constant substitution and minimal commenting can change test behavior.

