 % Source: Reviewer B, Detailed 3.
-\reviewcomment{2.3} \textbf{3. Potentially inaccurate characterization of SEDAR.} The paper characterizes SEDAR's comment-and-restore mechanism as ``destructive sanitization'' that ``sacrifices semantic depth.'' However, SEDAR's mechanism is designed to temporarily hide target-specific constructs from downstream fuzzers' strict parsers during mutation and restore them afterward. This is a pragmatic compatibility strategy, not a destructive removal of query logic.
+\reviewcomment{2.5} \textbf{3. Potentially inaccurate characterization of SEDAR.} The paper characterizes SEDAR's comment-and-restore mechanism as ``destructive sanitization'' that ``sacrifices semantic depth.'' However, SEDAR's mechanism is designed to temporarily hide target-specific constructs from downstream fuzzers' strict parsers during mutation and restore them afterward. This is a pragmatic compatibility strategy, not a destructive removal of query logic.

     The authors should revise their characterization to accurately reflect SEDAR's design intent and clarify the actual difference: MORPH performs pre-migration repair to make seeds parser-compatible, whereas SEDAR defers some compatibility handling until after mutation. A fairer comparison would evaluate both approaches under the same downstream fuzzer (e.g., GRIFFIN) and measure the net effect of each strategy on mutation effectiveness.

